            "presentation_state": "show_all",
            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "Gives the k largest values (or smallest), largest first, and their positions - without sorting everything. The two fastest joints, the five loudest frequency bins, the three nearest neighbours (smallest distances), the most likely classes of a classifier.\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.topk <k> <dim>\nDefaults: k 3, along the last dim. Giving a dim adds a dim box.\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ntensor in:\nThe values. Every input sends.\n\nk (int box, also an inlet):\nHow many. Asking for more than there are gives all of them.\n\ndim (int box, also an inlet; only when a dim was given):\nThe dim searched along: each slice gets its own k.\n\nlargest (checkbox, also an inlet):\nOn (the default), the largest; off, the smallest.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nvalues:\nThe k values, best first.\n\nindices:\nTheir positions. Sent first.\n\nThe answer stays on the input's device: a tensor on the GPU is worked on there and comes out there.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nFive joint speeds. The two fastest are 2.0 (joint 4) and 1.5 (joint 1).\n\nRELATED:\nt.sort orders all of them. t.argmax finds just the largest. np.top_k is the numpy version. t.index_select picks the rows the positions name.",
                    "value_type": "str"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 760,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
                    "name": "height",
                    "value": 1000,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "4": {
                    "name": "text_size",
                    "value": "30",
                    "value_type": "str"
                }
            },
            "text": "Gives the k largest values (or smallest), largest first, and their positions - without sorting everything. The two fastest joints, the five loudest frequency bins, the three nearest neighbours (smallest distances), the most likely classes of a classifier.\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.topk <k> <dim>\nDefaults: k 3, along the last dim. Giving a dim adds a dim box.\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ntensor in:\nThe values. Every input sends.\n\nk (int box, also an inlet):\nHow many. Asking for more than there are gives all of them.\n\ndim (int box, also an inlet; only when a dim was given):\nThe dim searched along: each slice gets its own k.\n\nlargest (checkbox, also an inlet):\nOn (the default), the largest; off, the smallest.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nvalues:\nThe k values, best first.\n\nindices:\nTheir positions. Sent first.\n\nThe answer stays on the input's device: a tensor on the GPU is worked on there and comes out there.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nFive joint speeds. The two fastest are 2.0 (joint 4) and 1.5 (joint 1).\n\nRELATED:\nt.sort orders all of them. t.argmax finds just the largest. np.top_k is the numpy version. t.index_select picks the rows the positions name."
        },
